{"candidates":[{"claim":"A decentralized network is NOT more resilient than a large redundant service when a shared external market signal synchronizes the independent operators into identical failure modes, creating self-organized criticality.","counterconditions":["If small operators are provably unaware of each other's market positions and use diverse, non-convergent scaling algorithms, the synchronization mechanism fails.","If the large service lacks internal architectural diversity and uses a single scaling policy, it fails in the same way as the synchronized network."],"mechanism":"Independent operators optimizing for the same market metrics (e.g., cost-per-request) converge on identical auto-scaling thresholds and configurations. During stable periods, they appear uncorrelated. However, under a specific market stress (e.g., a price spike in compute), all small services simultaneously hit their scaling limits and trigger the same cost-saving reboot/failover procedure. The shared cause (the market signal) induces correlated failure across 'independent' nodes, whereas a single large service can enforce internal diversity (different teams, codebases, and thresholds) to prevent synchronous behavior.","optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_008","SRC_001"],"typicality":0.95},{"claim":"The distinction between truly independent and merely uncorrelated failures is revealed by interventions on the postulated shared cause: truly independent failures will not systematically co-vary when a shared latent factor is perturbed, whereas synchronized failures will.","counterconditions":["If the shared cause is non-manipulable (e.g., a law of physics or a global macroeconomic event), the intervention cannot be performed.","If the system is so opaque that the shared latent variable cannot be identified, the test cannot be applied."],"mechanism":"If failures are merely uncorrelated but share a hidden common cause, manipulating that cause (e.g., artificially constraining a specific cloud provider's API) will force the independent failures to manifest as perfectly correlated. If the failures are truly independent, perturbing one node's environment will not predictably trigger failures in distant nodes. This follows a counterfactual logic: if we had changed the shared input, would the failures have been different?","optional_refs":["SRC_004","SRC_007"],"typicality":0.85},{"claim":"Algorithmic convergence on identical training data and models constitutes a shared cause of failure, making a decentralized network of AI-operated services strictly less resilient than a large service with diverse, human-overseen subsystems.","counterconditions":["If small services use locally generated, private training data that is not shared, the convergence mechanism fails.","If the large service also uses a single shared training set for all its sub-services, it loses this advantage."],"mechanism":"When small services independently train their operational models on the same public datasets (e.g., the same cloud provider's telemetry), they learn identical failure-detection heuristics. They will all misclassify the same novel anomaly as benign, leading to simultaneous cascading failures. The shared training data is the shared cause. The large service, having 'strictly more total internal redundancy,' can afford to train diverse models on proprietary, artificially noised, or synthetic data, creating an epistemic firewall.","optional_refs":["SRC_008","SRC_006","SRC_002"],"typicality":0.75},{"claim":"A decentralized network is more resilient than a single large service because the large service's internal redundancy is subject to a shared administrative and operational sphere that acts as a single point of failure for failure-mode diversity.","counterconditions":["If the decentralized operators all outsource their administration to the same third-party (e.g., the same cloud provider), the administrative shared cause reappears.","If the large service can maintain strictly siloed administrative teams with zero shared dependencies, the mechanism fails."],"mechanism":"Even if a large service has logically isolated sub-services, they share a common administrative backbone (e.g., one HR department, one security policy, one deployment pipeline). A failure in this shared administrative layer (e.g., a bad security patch pushed to all internal sub-services) can simultaneously compromise all internal redundancy. The decentralized network, lacking this shared administrative backbone, is immune to this specific class of correlated failure. The shared cause is not technical but organizational.","optional_refs":["SRC_007","SRC_005"],"typicality":0.65},{"claim":"The distinction between independent and uncorrelated failures is epistemological, not ontological: we can only observe a failure as 'independent' until a shared cause is discovered, meaning the resilience of decentralization is always a temporary, revisable hypothesis.","counterconditions":["If a complete physical model of all interactions is available, independence can be established definitively, making the distinction ontological.","If one adopts a strictly instrumentalist view, the distinction is meaningless and only the observed correlation matters."],"mechanism":"There is no finite set of observations that can prove true independence, as there could always be an unobserved shared cause (e.g., a shared cosmic ray source, a shared supply chain vulnerability). Therefore, the claim of independence is a theoretical commitment, not an empirical fact. Evidence distinguishes 'uncorrelated' from 'independent' only by shifting the burden of proof: we treat failures as independent until a shared cause is found, at which point we revise our model. Resilience is thus a function of our ignorance, not just the system's structure.","optional_refs":["SRC_003","SRC_001"],"typicality":0.3}],"scratch_proposal":{"links":[{"from_ref":"NEW_001","relation_hint":"The 'leader bird' is a specific instance of the 'external market signal' that synchronizes the independent operators.","to_ref":"SCR_001"}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_003","SRC_008"],"content":"If a large service is like a single organism with redundant organs, and a decentralized network is like a flock of birds, the flock seems more resilient. But what if all birds are following the same leader bird? Then the flock is effectively one organism. The shared cause is the leader. The large service (the organism) might actually be more resilient if it has multiple 'brains' that disagree. The question is: does the decentralized network have a 'leader' it doesn't know about?","why_keep_this":"This frames the algorithmic convergence idea in a biological analogy, which might make it easier to generate testable hypotheses about 'leader' signals in decentralized networks."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can we design a 'leaderless' protocol for decentralized services that provably prevents them from synchronizing on shared external signals?","related_refs":["SCR_001","NEW_001","SRC_005"]}]}}